  "titleEn": "Braille",
  "titleEs": "Braille",
  "titleText": {
    "es": "La única diferencia importante que he visto está en los colores. Cuando el texto normal dice \"pulsar el botón rojo\", el texto en braille dice \"pulsar el botón de dos centímetros\".",
    "en": "The only big difference I've seen is in colors. Where the regular text reads “press red button,” the braille reads “press two-inch button.”",
    "noteEn": "The Spanish archive localizes the original two-inch measurement as two centimeters."
  },
  "cardDefinitions": [
    {
      "id": "word-que--after-hace-time",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "que",
      "answerEn": "since; no separate English word in a natural ‘a while ago’ translation",
      "noteEn": "After hace and an amount of elapsed time, que can connect that time to the event being dated. English often restructures the idea rather than translating que by itself.",
      "example": { "es": "Hace un mes que hablé con Ana.", "en": "I spoke with Ana a month ago." },
      "tags": ["word", "time", "connector", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-leer", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "leer", "answerEn": "to read", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "verb", "A1"] },
    { "id": "word-braille", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "braille", "answerEn": "Braille", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "writing system", "accessibility"] },
    {
      "id": "word-dado--darse-cuenta",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "dado",
      "answerEn": "realized; noticed (as part of me he dado cuenta)",
      "noteEn": "Dado is the completed-action form of dar, normally ‘given.’ Inside darse cuenta, the whole expression means ‘to realize’ or ‘to notice,’ so dado is not translated as ‘given.’",
      "example": { "es": "Me he dado cuenta del error.", "en": "I have noticed the mistake." },
      "tags": ["word", "fixed expression", "present perfect", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-de--sign-content",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "de",
      "answerEn": "on; belonging to (linking content to a sign or notice)",
      "noteEn": "Spanish can use de to connect a message or wording to the sign that contains it. Natural English often says that the message is ‘on’ the sign.",
      "example": { "es": "El mensaje del cartel es muy claro.", "en": "The message on the sign is very clear." },
      "tags": ["word", "preposition", "signs", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-carteles", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "carteles", "answerEn": "signs; posters", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "noun", "A1"] },
    { "id": "word-concuerdan", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "concuerdan", "answerEn": "they match; they agree", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "verb", "agreement", "A2"] },
    {
      "id": "word-con--concordar-complement",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "con",
      "answerEn": "no separate English word here; links concordar to what matches",
      "noteEn": "Concordar uses con before the person, statement, or thing that agrees or matches. English may translate concordar con simply as ‘to match.’",
      "example": { "es": "La copia no concuerda con el original.", "en": "The copy does not match the original." },
      "tags": ["word", "verb complement", "agreement", "A2"]
    },
    { "id": "word-texto", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "texto", "answerEn": "text; wording", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "noun", "A1"] },
    { "id": "word-normal--ordinary", "kind": "word", "promptEs": "normal", "answerEn": "regular; ordinary", "noteEn": "", "tags": ["word", "adjective", "A1"] },
    {
      "id": "word-tercera",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "tercera",
      "answerEn": "third (feminine singular)",
      "noteEn": "Tercera is the feminine form of tercero, ‘third.’ Its ending matches a feminine singular noun such as planta or puerta.",
      "example": { "es": "Es la tercera puerta.", "en": "It is the third door." },
      "tags": ["word", "ordinal", "agreement", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-l-o-s--spelled-los",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "l-o-s",
      "answerEn": "the (masculine plural; the letters spell los)",
      "noteEn": "Hyphens between individual letters show a word being read out one letter at a time. Read l-o-s together as los.",
      "example": { "es": "C-A-S-A forma la palabra casa.", "en": "C-A-S-A spells the word casa." },
      "tags": ["word", "spelling", "article", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-v-i-d-e-n-t-e-s--spelled-sighted-people",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "v-i-d-e-n-t-e-s",
      "answerEn": "sighted people; people who can see (spelled out)",
      "noteEn": "Read the separated letters together as videntes. Vidente can also mean a seer or clairvoyant, but here it contrasts people who can see with people who read Braille by touch.",
      "example": { "es": "La señal tiene texto para personas videntes.", "en": "The sign has text for sighted people." },
      "tags": ["word", "spelling", "accessibility", "polysemy"]
    },
    {
      "id": "word-a-p-e-s-t-a-n--spelled-figurative",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "a-p-e-s-t-a-n",
      "answerEn": "they suck; they are awful (spelled out, informal)",
      "noteEn": "Read the separated letters together as apestan. The literal meaning is ‘they stink’; informally, the same verb can say that people or things are terrible.",
      "example": { "es": "Esas reglas apestan.", "en": "Those rules suck." },
      "tags": ["word", "spelling", "figurative language", "informal"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-aprender-a-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "aprender a + verbo en su forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone learns how to do an activity?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of aprender, then a, then the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "Only aprender changes for the person and time. The action after a stays in its dictionary form—the form ending in -ar, -er, or -ir. English normally uses ‘learn to’ before the action.",
      "example": { "es": "Marta aprende a nadar.", "en": "Marta is learning to swim." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "verb pattern", "learning", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-concordar-con",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "concordar con + persona, idea o cosa",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that one thing agrees with or matches another?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of concordar followed by con and the person, idea, or thing it matches.",
      "noteEn": "Concordar changes for who or what agrees. Con introduces the other side of the match. Depending on context, English can use ‘agree with,’ ‘match,’ or ‘be consistent with.’",
      "example": { "es": "Los datos concuerdan con el informe.", "en": "The data match the report." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "verb pattern", "agreement", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-ordinal-before-noun-agreement",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "número ordinal antes de un sustantivo",
      "questionEn": "How do you use words such as first, second, or third before a Spanish noun?",
      "answerEn": "Put the ordinal before the noun and make its ending match the noun's gender and number.",
      "noteEn": "An ordinal gives a position in an order. Primero, segundo, tercero, and similar words usually come before the noun. Most forms use -o with a masculine singular noun and -a with a feminine singular noun, but primero and tercero shorten to primer and tercer before a masculine singular noun. Plural nouns use plural forms.",
      "example": { "es": "La segunda puerta está en el tercer piso.", "en": "The second door is on the third floor." },
      "tags": ["grammar", "ordinals", "agreement", "A1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "concept-braille-hidden-message",
      "kind": "concept",
      "promptEs": "el mensaje oculto en braille",
      "answerEn": "Braille and printed lettering can encode different messages on the same sign.",
      "noteEn": "The joke relies on many sighted readers being unable to check the raised-dot text. A sign can therefore look ordinary in print while its Braille carries a private, hostile message.",
      "tags": ["concept", "Braille", "accessibility", "joke"]
    }
  ],
